  "id": "DrugBank_DB13998 - MONDO_grouped_10604_10602_15720_15719_18660_15721_15715_15716_15717",
  "verbalization": "Lonoctocog Alfa is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hemophilia through a series of enzymatic pathways that ultimately lead to the production of antihemophilic factor (AF), a critical component for clot formation in hemostasis. The evidence suggests that Lonoctocog Alfa, through its enzymatic activity, catalyzes the synthesis of Proc, which in turn is converted into Antihemophilic Factor, Human Recombinant, and Moroctocog Alfa. These compounds are known to be used in the treatment of Hemophilia, indicating a direct therapeutic relationship. The scores from the paths suggest a moderate level of confidence in the mechanistic link, with the most direct pathway involving the conversion of Proc into Antihemophilic Factor, Human Recombinant, which is explicitly associated with Hemophilia. The presence of multiple pathways converging on the same target (Proc) and the shared indication for Hemophilia across different pathways supports the hypothesis that Lonoctocog Alfa may be repurposed for Hemophilia treatment. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of enzymatic conversion in the production of therapeutic agents, reinforcing the potential for Lonoctocog Alfa to be used in the management of Hemophilia.",
